#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.133.0 — Payroll 5: Variable Pay — Overtime, Bonuses,
Commissions & Deductions (pass 5 of 8).

Variable pay governed, not improvised: the additional-salary
machinery, overtime through one channel, bonuses as documents,
commissions computed from selling documents, arrears travelling
forward, staff lending mechanics, and off-cycle payments as
mini-runs. Manual depth from birth: 9 chapters, 35-bank.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_payroll_pro.seed_payroll_pro_track

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.132.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_payroll_pro_data.json"
SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_payroll_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Variable pay: governed, not improvised", "12", "<p>The structure computes the same numbers every month — and every month, real life adds to them: the overtime worked, the bonus earned, the commission closed, the correction owed, the loan advancing. This module governs the layer where those one-offs live: <b>variable pay</b> — and its thesis is the line this track has drawn since module 1: <b>variable pay is governed, not improvised.</b></p><p><b>Why this layer is where structure leaks.</b> The diagnosis first, because the consultant will meet it everywhere: the FIXED pay is easy to govern (the structure holds it; the review changes it; nobody types it) — but the variable layer is where every governance instinct meets its temptation: the bonus is discretionary (so why paper it?), the overtime is small (so why gate it?), the correction is urgent (so why wait?), the advance is a kindness (so why policy it?) — and the trade's payrolls answer those four questions wrongly for years until the layer is a parallel economy: cash envelopes beside the bank run, commissions computed from a salesperson's own notebook, corrections negotiated at the counter, and lending that is really the founder's personal favour book. The cost is every disease this academy has named at once — the favour system (unevenness denominated in naira), the version drift (the slip that doesn't know about the envelope), the tax leak (the cash bonus module 2's projection never saw), and the fraud surface (the variable layer is where the padding, the phantom overtime, and the invented one-off live, because it is the layer where numbers are typed).</p><p><b>The cure: the additional-salary machinery.</b> One instrument governs the whole layer: every variable item is a DOCUMENT — the additional-pay entry carrying its TYPE (the component it posts to — module 1's variable components receiving their population), its AMOUNT (or the computation that produces it — Chapters 2 and 4's derived items), its AUTHORITY (who approved, per the type's approval rule — the spot bonus's approver is named policy, not ambient generosity), its PERIOD (the run that will carry it — the cutoff of module 3 governing admission), and its REFERENCE (the source it derives from — the overtime sheet, the scheme, the arrears computation, the instrument). The document then flows the run's ritual like everything else: validated (module 3's pending-document read), computed (onto the slip as its labelled line — module 4's anatomy), posted (to its component's ledger address), and archived (the variable layer's history as readable as the fixed layer's — which is the whole point: the one-off that is a document is one-off in AMOUNT but standard in GOVERNANCE).</p><p><b>The module's map.</b> The variable earnings first — overtime (Chapter 2), bonuses (Chapter 3), commissions (Chapter 4 — the Selling track's crossover); the corrections layer — arrears and adjustments travelling forward (Chapter 5 — module 3's frozen-run law cashing its promise); the lending layer — loans and advances at mechanics depth (Chapter 6 — module 4's consent layer receiving its machinery); and the off-cycle payments that leave the monthly ritual without leaving governance (Chapter 7). One case study, one pattern set, one law — and the thread throughout: the variable layer run as documents is the difference between a payroll that flexes and a payroll that leaks.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Overtime: hours into money, one channel", "13", "<p>The commonest variable earning, and the one whose governance the run already settled — this chapter completes it: the policy, the channel, the approval, and the read. The overtime chapter.</p><p><b>The policy first.</b> Overtime exists as a POLICY before it exists as a payment: WHO is eligible (role-based — the operational roles whose extra hours are compensable, distinguished from the salaried-inclusive roles whose contracts absorb reasonable extension: the line the adviser confirms and the contracts state, because the eligibility argued case-by-case is the fairness leak at its most frequent), the RATE (the multiplier convention — the policy stating its multiple of the derived hourly rate, with the hourly rate COMPUTED from the structure: the monthly-salary-to-hourly derivation stated once — the divisor convention explicit — so every overtime naira traces to the structure like everything else), the CAPS and authorisations (the monthly overtime ceiling per person that flags the roster problem before it becomes a budget problem — Chapter's read below), and the WEEKEND/holiday treatments where policy differentiates them (stated, not remembered).</p><p><b>The one channel.</b> Module 3's case-law rule, now with its full reasoning: overtime enters payroll through the ATTENDANCE PACKAGE only — the hours captured by the HR track's machinery (the check-ins that bound the shift, the overtime approved against the captured hours), closed in the attendance close, and delivered in the monthly cargo — never through a side list, a supervisor's text, or a direct additional-pay entry (the double-counting HealthTrade met in module 3 was exactly two channels carrying one truth; the fix was the channel count, not the vigilance). The discipline's dividend: overtime hours are FACTS from the capture layer (disputed hours resolved by the check-in trail, not by recollection), and the padding fraud's surface shrinks to the approval step — which is why the approval is designed next.</p><p><b>Approval: before the hours, not after the fact.</b> The governance's timing rule: overtime is APPROVED IN ADVANCE as the norm (the supervisor authorising the extension before it is worked — the roster's coverage decision made consciously, with the cost visible), with the discovered-overtime path as the governed exception (the emergency extension worked first and regularised within the window — module 3 of the HR track's regularisation discipline applying, bounded and reasoned), because after-the-fact overtime as the NORM is unmanaged cost plus unmanageable verification (the hours already worked can only be disputed, never declined — the approval that can only say yes is not an approval), and the approver's economics are taught plainly: the overtime hour costs its multiple; the roster fix costs a conversation — Chapter's read makes the comparison monthly.</p><p><b>The overtime read.</b> The layer's altitude discipline, closing the chapter: overtime read monthly at the pattern level — the CHRONIC overtime (the same role, every month, is not a surge; it is a staffing gap wearing a premium rate — the HR track's headcount conversation triggered by payroll's number: the trilogy's reads meshing), the CLUSTERED overtime (one supervisor's team consistently authorising heavy hours is a management read — the padding question asked politely by the data: the hours against the capture trail, the approvals against the pattern, module 3's session-variance epistemics applied to hours), the SEASONAL overtime (the festive-season surge that is real, planned, and budgeted — the calendar's known shape, distinguished from drift), and the ZERO overtime in roles that plainly extend (the unrecorded extension is either goodwill burning out or hours moving through a side channel — both worth finding). The read's one-line summary for the owner's page: overtime is a price signal — it prices the roster's gaps, and the firm that reads it staffs deliberately while the firm that doesn't pays time-and-a-half for its planning debt.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Bonuses: the discretionary layer, governed", "13", "<p>The variable earning with the widest discretion and therefore the strongest need for governance: the bonus — kept generous AND governed, because the two are not opposites. The bonus chapter.</p><p><b>The bonus taxonomy.</b> The types, each with its governance shape: the <b>performance bonus</b> (fed by the HR track's appraisal machinery — the rating-linked payout where policy runs one, computed from the calibrated outcome per the published rule: the bonus that traces to the folder is an incentive; the one that traces to a mood is a lottery, and the workforce knows which it is playing within two cycles), the <b>festive/thirteenth-month payment</b> (where the firm runs one — a POLICY, stated in the terms, scheduled as Chapter 7's second run, and budgeted from January, because the thirteenth month discovered in November is a cashflow ambush the firm aimed at itself), the <b>spot bonus</b> (the immediate recognition — the generator sorted, the save at the counter — governed lightest but governed: the named approvers, the per-instance cap, the document with its reason: HealthTrade's ₦30k in module 1 was exactly this machinery, thirty seconds of process), and the <b>scheme payouts</b> beyond (the target bonuses, the retention payments — each per its written rule).</p><p><b>Every bonus a document.</b> The machinery applied: the bonus as an additional-pay entry with its type, authority, and reason — landing on the slip as its LABELLED line (module 4's anatomy: the bonus the worker can see and name is recognition twice — once in naira, once in respect; the mystery credit is recognition wasted), posted to its component's address (the bonus expense visible in the cost-center P&L — Accounts 6 seeing what generosity costs where), and TAXED correctly (the bonus is taxable income — module 2's annual projection absorbing it in its month, the PAYE spike explained by the frame the payroll seat can narrate: the bonus month's tax question is Chapter 5 of module 4's catalogued query, pre-answered by the announcement that accompanies the payout). The cash-envelope alternative convicted with its full bill: the off-book bonus is a tax exposure (the income module 2's computation never saw), an evenness breach (the envelope has no policy), a ledger hole (the cash that left without an address), and a precedent (the envelope's recipient learned where generosity lives — outside the system, which is where they will look for it next time).</p><p><b>Policy versus gift.</b> The chapter's governing distinction, drawn once and used forever: the bonus that is POLICY (the thirteenth month, the scheme payout, the rating-linked amount) is an ENTITLEMENT once its conditions are met — administered evenly, computed from its rule, disputed against its rule (the HR track's entitlement law reaching the discretionary layer: what the policy grants, the mood cannot withhold) — while the bonus that is GIFT (the spot recognition, the discretionary year-end) is discretion EXERCISED within governance (the named approver, the cap, the document — discretion about WHO and HOW MUCH, never about whether the machinery applies). The confusion of the two is the layer's classic wound in both directions: the 'discretionary' thirteenth month withheld in a tight year from staff whose contracts state it (an entitlement breached, with the adviser about to be involved), and the spot bonus institutionalised by repetition until its absence is a grievance (the gift that hardened into an expectation nobody priced — the consultant's advice: the recurring gift should either become policy deliberately or vary visibly, because the third path, the accidental entitlement, is chosen by default and paid for annually).</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Commissions: the selling crossover", "14", "<p>The variable earning that joins two certifications: the commission — earned at the counter the Selling track governs, paid through the run this track governs, and computed from DOCUMENTS at both ends. The crossover chapter.</p><p><b>The scheme is written.</b> The commission exists as a SCHEME before anyone sells: the written rule stating the BASE (commission on WHAT — the invoiced sale, the collected payment, the margin: the base decision is the scheme's whole character, and the Selling track taught its consequences — the invoice-based scheme pays for sales; the collection-based scheme pays for MONEY, and the trade's receivables discipline usually argues for the latter or a split), the RATE structure (flat, tiered, threshold-gated — per the written table), the PERIOD (the month or quarter it computes over), the ELIGIBILITY (the roles in the scheme, the joiner's proration, the leaver's treatment — Chapter's clause below), and the CAPS and gates where the design uses them (the target gate, the ceiling). The scheme signed and filed like the structure it is — because the commission dispute without a written scheme is two recollections negotiating, and the salesperson's version is always, honestly, larger.</p><p><b>Computed from source documents.</b> The discipline that makes commission a computation instead of a negotiation: the commission derives from the SELLING TRACK'S DOCUMENTS — the invoices, the collections, the credit notes — pulled by the scheme's rule over the period (the sales register's numbers, not the salesperson's notebook and not the manager's memory: the two tracks' machinery joining at the computation, which is why the consultant who holds both certifications configures this in an hour while the single-track consultant mediates it forever), the computation GENERATED as the additional-pay document with its reference (the period's source list attached — the commission's provenance as walkable as the slip's), and the anti-pattern named for every cleanup engagement: commission-from-memory (the month-end negotiation over a notebook) is the variable layer's version-drift disease at its most contentious, because it monthly re-litigates the firm's biggest incentive.</p><p><b>The clawback clause.</b> The scheme's hard edge, designed in daylight: the commission paid on the sale that REVERSES (the returned goods, the cancelled invoice, the collection that bounces) — governed by the scheme's own clause (the reversal netting against the period's computation, or the clawback recovering per the stated rule — decided at design, stated in the scheme, consented per module 4's layer), because the clawback IMPROVISED at the first big return is a grievance with a calculator (the salesperson who was never told the rule experiences the recovery as confiscation — module 4's never-surprised law applying to the downside exactly as to the upside), and the netting mechanics kept honest (the reversal visible in the commission statement's lines, not silently shrinking a total).</p><p><b>The commission statement.</b> The layer's transparency instrument, closing the chapter: each period's commission accompanied by its STATEMENT — the source documents listed (the invoices/collections in the base), the rule applied visibly (the tier reached, the gate passed), the reversals netted with their references, and the payout reconciling to the slip's line — the salesperson's own reconciliation document (module 4's answered-by-pointing, pre-delivered: the seller who can audit their own commission does, monthly, for free — twenty-seven personal auditors' logic at the incentive layer), and the statement's quiet second service: the commission data read at altitude joins the Selling track's scheme reads (the scheme that pays out against flat sales has a base problem; the seller earning far off the pack's curve is either the star the firm should study or the anomaly the firm should audit — both worth knowing, neither visible without the statement's data). The crossover's summary, for the certification's sales pitch: commission done this way costs one configuration and zero monthly arguments — and the firm's biggest incentive finally points exactly where the scheme aimed it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Arrears & adjustments: corrections travel forward", "13", "<p>Module 3 froze the run and promised the corrections a home; this chapter is the home: the arrears and adjustments layer — where errors are corrected honestly, visibly, and FORWARD. The corrections chapter.</p><p><b>The underpayment: arrears.</b> The correction owed TO the worker (the missed increment, the dropped overtime hour, the validation miss surfacing late): governed as a document — the ARREARS entry carrying its computation SHOWN (the difference derived: what should have computed, what did, the delta per period — module 1's walk applied to the gap, because the arrears figure asserted without its arithmetic is a new dispute wearing a correction), its periods identified (which sealed months it repairs — referenced, never reopened: the frozen runs stand; the repair lands in the current run as its labelled line), its authority (the correction approved like any variable item), and its COMMUNICATION (module 4's law: the arrears announced to its recipient with the honest account — <i>we found it, here is the arithmetic, here is when it lands</i> — the error converted into the trust deposit module 4 described). The tax note carried with it: the arrears is income in its payment month (module 2's projection absorbing it — the spike explained in the announcement, pre-empting the query).</p><p><b>The backdated change: computed, not estimated.</b> The commonest arrears species, given its machinery: the increment or promotion effective in a PAST month (the review that concluded late, the movement processed after its date — HR 7's effective dates meeting module 3's cutoffs): the system computes the backdated difference PER PERIOD from the assignment's effective date (the months between the effective date and the processing date each re-derived against the new structure — the machinery's arithmetic, not a lump-sum estimate), lands it as the arrears line with its breakdown, and the discipline's contrast is the cleanup engagement's daily find: the backdated raise 'settled' with a round number nobody can derive — generous or short, nobody knows, and the not-knowing is the injury.</p><p><b>The overpayment: the recovery conversation.</b> The correction owed BY the worker — the harder direction, governed accordingly: the overpayment established with its arithmetic (the same shown computation, both directions honest), the CONVERSATION before the deduction (module 4's never-surprised law at its most necessary — the recovery discovered on a slip is an ambush with the firm's error as its author: the person told, the arithmetic walked, the recovery agreed), the recovery CONSENTED and SCHEDULED (module 4's instrument layer — the repayment plan signed, spread per the cap's protection: the firm's error does not license the worker's hardship; the recovery of the firm's mistake rides the same percentage cap as every deduction, which sometimes means the firm's error is recovered over months, and that patience is the price of its own mistake), and the write-off path honest where the arithmetic or equity argues it (the small stale overpayment written off by authority — Accounts 4's write-off discipline, payroll edition — beats the pursuit that costs more than it recovers in naira and far more in trust).</p><p><b>The adjustment's visibility.</b> The layer's slip discipline, closing the chapter: every correction VISIBLE as what it is (the arrears line labelled with its nature and periods, the recovery line with its reference and balance — module 4's anatomy receiving the corrections layer), never smuggled (the correction folded silently into a component's amount is the version drift this track buried — the slip that quietly differs from its structure is unexplainable by design, and unexplainable is the one thing this track's slips are never allowed to be). The corrections layer run this way completes module 3's promise: the frozen run and the honest correction are one system — March means March forever, AND every error March contained has a dated, derived, visible repair. That pairing is what 'corrections travel forward' means, and it is the only version of payroll integrity that survives contact with real months.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Staff lending: loans & advances, the mechanics", "13", "<p>Module 4 papered the consent; this chapter builds the machine: the staff lending layer — the policy, the instruments, the flows, and the book. The lending chapter.</p><p><b>The policy.</b> Lending exists as POLICY before the first request (the favour disease's final habitat, fenced in advance): ELIGIBILITY (who may borrow — the confirmation gate, the tenure threshold: the probationer's loan is a risk the policy prices deliberately or declines), LIMITS (the ceiling as a multiple of salary — the affordability logic stated: the loan the recovery cap cannot service inside a reasonable schedule is declined by arithmetic, not by mood), the SCHEDULE conventions (the standard tenors, the recovery rate defaults — inside module 4's percentage cap always), the INTEREST position (interest-free as welfare or priced per policy — a decision with tax and fairness dimensions the adviser and accountant weigh once; whichever is chosen applies evenly), and the APPROVAL route (the requests through the standing flow to the named authority — the founder's personal favour book retired into a policy the payroll seat can administer without courage).</p><p><b>The two instruments.</b> The distinction the layer runs on: the <b>salary advance</b> (THIS month's pay, early — bounded by the earned-to-date logic, recovered in FULL from the next run: the short bridge, governed lightly — the request, the approval, the off-cycle disbursement where urgency demands it, the single recovery; the advance's discipline is its brevity, and the advance that rolls month to month is not an advance but an undocumented loan wearing one — converted formally or closed) and the <b>staff loan</b> (the multi-month instrument — module 4's full consent layer: the agreement with amount, schedule, recovery rate, early-settlement terms, and the separation clause; the DISBURSEMENT through the payment separations like any money movement; the RECOVERY as the governed component advancing per schedule, its balance on every slip; the settlement ceremony at the end). The flows differ; the governance constant: instrument, then announcement, then slip.</p><p><b>The ledger's mirror.</b> The lending layer's accounting, walked once: the disbursement creating the RECEIVABLE (the staff-loan asset account — the firm's money, out, per person: Accounts 4's receivable discipline with employees as the counterparty), the recoveries reducing it run by run (the payroll journal's recovery credits landing against the receivable — module 3's posting anatomy extended one address), the BOOK reconciling monthly (the per-person balances against the slips' balance lines against the ledger's account — three views, one truth, agreeing by construction because they share one parent), and the aged read applying (the loan whose recoveries stalled — the leaver's unsettled balance, the paused schedule — surfaced by the same aging discipline every receivable gets).</p><p><b>The book read.</b> The layer's altitude view, closing the chapter: the lending book read quarterly — the EXPOSURE total (the firm's capital in staff loans, sized against policy's comfort: the book that grows past its ceiling is treasury drift wearing welfare), the DISTRIBUTION (the concentration read — the book that is mostly two names is a different risk than the book that is thin and wide), and the pattern the consultant teaches the owner to read with care: the CHRONIC borrower — the advance every month, the loan atop the loan — is a WELFARE SIGNAL before it is a credit risk (the salary that never reaches month's end has a story: the wage question, the family shock, the debt spiral — the HR track's check-in conversation triggered by payroll's pattern, handled with the dignity the HR track taught), because the lending layer, read humanely, is one of the firm's few honest windows into its people's financial weather — and the firm that notices early helps early, which is both the right thing and the cheaper thing, in that order.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Off-cycle payments: outside the ritual, inside governance", "13", "<p>The monthly ritual is the rule; this chapter governs the exceptions — the payments that must move OUTSIDE the run without moving outside governance. The off-cycle chapter.</p><p><b>When off-cycle is legitimate.</b> The catalogue, closed: the MISSED payment (the bounced credit's re-payment — module 3's resolution flow already runs this), the URGENT advance (Chapter 6's bridge, where the payday is too far), the EXIT settlement that cannot wait for the run (module 7's terrain — the final entitlements where timing or terms demand independence), the ERROR whose correction genuinely cannot ride the next run (rare, and the rarity is the point: the arrears layer of Chapter 5 exists precisely so most corrections CAN wait honestly — the off-cycle correction is for the missed-salary class of error, not the missed-allowance class), and the SCHEDULED second runs (the thirteenth month, the festive payout — the planned off-cycles that are really additional ritual runs on the calendar). Everything else waits — and the waiting is defensible because the arrears machinery makes it whole.</p><p><b>The off-cycle as a mini-run.</b> The governance rule: the off-cycle payment runs the SAME ritual at small scale — the document (the additional-pay entry or settlement with its authority), the computation (from the machinery, not a typed net — the off-cycle that hand-computes a net has bypassed the statutory layer, and module 2's projection meets it as an error in the annual true-up), the SEPARATED HANDS (the preparer-approver-releaser separation does not shrink because the amount did — the off-cycle is fraud's preferred vehicle precisely because it travels alone, outside the month's variance read: the single payment with unified hands is the exact shape of the invented one-off), the POSTING (to the same addresses, dated its date), and the SLIP treatment (the off-cycle payment slipped or statement-ed per its nature — the mid-month advance appearing on the month's slip as its recovery's twin, the exit settlement carrying module 7's full statement: nothing moves money to a person without a document the person can read).</p><p><b>The scheduled second run.</b> The legitimate off-cycle at its largest: the thirteenth-month or festive run — planned as a RUN (the calendar's known date, the population defined by the policy, the computation from the rule — the month's-basic or the stated formula, taxed per module 2's treatment of the payment in its month), budgeted from January (the accrual conversation with the accountant — Accounts 4's provision logic: the December obligation built monthly is a line; discovered in November it is a crisis), and communicated per module 4 (the announcement with the arithmetic — the festive payment's net effect shown, because the thirteenth month's PAYE interaction is the season's guaranteed query, pre-answered).</p><p><b>The off-cycle read.</b> The layer's health metric, closing the chapter: the off-cycle COUNT read quarterly — the near-zero count is the ritual working (the calendar absorbing the firm's real payment needs; the exceptions rare and each explained), while the CLIMBING count is a diagnosis (the frequent missed-payment off-cycles indict the bank-detail hygiene or the funding discipline; the frequent urgent advances indict the payday's position against the month's shape or the workforce's financial weather — Chapter 6's welfare read arriving by another door; the frequent 'couldn't wait' corrections indict the run's own quality gates), and the consultant's framing for the owner completes the module's arc: the off-cycle layer is the ritual's exception log, and a ritual whose exceptions are rare, documented, and boring is a ritual that has actually won — which is the standard this track set in module 3 and holds to the end.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's variable year", "16", "<p>The variable layer, governed through a full year. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>Overtime settles (Ch. 2).</b> The one-channel rule holds from module 3's fix; the policy lands beside it: eligibility by role (the counter, warehouse, and driver roles in; the salaried supervisors out — stated in the contracts at the next renewal cycle), the rate convention published, approvals in advance as the norm. The read earns its keep in month three: the Ibadan warehouse's chronic overtime — the same two names, every month — is walked as a staffing gap, not a padding case (the capture trail clean, the approvals honest, the hours real): the roster conversation adds a part-time Saturday hire, and the overtime line falls by 60% — <i>we were paying time-and-a-half for a vacancy</i>, the MD's note reads, which is Chapter 2's price-signal lesson verbatim.</p><p><b>Bonuses & the thirteenth month (Ch. 3, 7).</b> The spot-bonus machinery (module 1's generator case) gets its policy: two named approvers, a per-instance cap, the document with its reason — eleven spot bonuses in the year, each a labelled slip line. The festive payment is formalised the other way: the December payment the firm had made 'when the year allowed' for six years running is recognised for what it had become — the accidental entitlement — and the MD chooses deliberately: the thirteenth month enters policy (stated terms, defined population), the accrual line starts in JANUARY (the accountant's provision building monthly — <i>the first December that isn't a surprise to our own books</i>), and the scheduled second run executes on its announced date with the net-effect announcement pre-answering the PAYE question at the branches.</p><p><b>The commission quarter (Ch. 4).</b> The two salespeople move onto a written scheme: collection-based (the receivables discipline arguing for money over paper — the Selling track's logic applied), tiered, quarterly, with the clawback clause stated and signed. The computation generates from the collection register — the first quarter's statements land beside the payout slips (each seller's source list, tier, and total — one seller finds a missing collection in her own statement's list; the correction runs forward and the channel's credibility is purchased exactly as module 4 taught). The clause earns its keep in quarter two: a ₦480,000 collection bounces after the payout — the reversal nets against the running quarter per the clause, visible in the statement's lines, and the conversation is arithmetic, not argument: <i>the rule we signed is the rule that ran.</i></p><p><b>Corrections & lending (Ch. 5-6).</b> The review cycle's one late conclusion becomes the backdated-increment case: effective March, processed May — the machinery derives the two months' delta per period, the arrears line lands with its breakdown, the announcement carries the arithmetic. One overpayment surfaces (a proration miss on a mid-month joiner — the firm's error): the conversation first, the arithmetic walked, the recovery consented over four months inside the cap — <i>your mistake, my schedule</i>, the employee notes, accurately, and the file agrees. The lending book's first year: policy adopted (confirmation gate, salary-multiple ceiling, interest-free by choice, even by rule), five loans and nine advances administered through the flow, the book reconciling three-ways monthly — and one chronic-advance pattern (the same till operator, four consecutive months) handled as Chapter 6 taught: the welfare conversation before the credit decision, surfacing a family medical situation — the response a governed hardship loan and the HR track's support conversation, the pattern read as a signal, not a nuisance. The year's off-cycle count: six — four bounced-credit re-payments, one urgent advance, one exit settlement — each documented, each boring. The MD's closing note: <i>the one-offs used to be where the system ended; now they are just the part of it that moves.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "13", "<p>The variable layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the cash envelope.</b> Symptom: bonuses beside the bank run; generosity the ledger never saw; the tax the projection never computed. Disease: the off-book variable layer (Ch. 1, 3) — a parallel economy with every disease at once. Fix: every bonus a document through the machinery — labelled on the slip, posted to its address, taxed in its month — generosity kept AND governed.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — commission from memory.</b> Symptom: the month-end notebook negotiation; the firm's biggest incentive re-litigated monthly; the star seller's version always larger. Disease: the scheme unwritten and the computation sourceless (Ch. 4). Fix: the written scheme, the computation from selling documents, the statement as the seller's own reconciliation — one configuration, zero arguments.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the overtime leak.</b> Symptom: chronic hours nobody questions; the padding nobody can disprove; time-and-a-half as a standing budget line. Disease: no policy, side channels, after-the-fact approval (Ch. 2). Fix: the role-based policy, the one channel from the capture layer, approval before hours, and the monthly read — overtime as a price signal, answered with staffing.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the clawback surprise.</b> Symptom: the recovery discovered on a slip after a big return; the confiscation grievance; the scheme's downside improvised in anger. Disease: the reversal unclause-d (Ch. 4) and the never-surprised law breached (Ch. 5). Fix: the clawback designed in daylight, stated in the signed scheme, netted visibly — and every recovery, of any kind, conversationed before deducted.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the rolling advance.</b> Symptom: the advance every month, silently renewed; the undocumented loan wearing a bridge's name; the chronic borrower unnoticed. Disease: the instruments confused and the pattern unread (Ch. 6). Fix: the advance recovered in full next run or converted formally; the book read quarterly; the chronic pattern met as a welfare signal first — noticed early, helped early.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the off-cycle as normal.</b> Symptom: payments moving whenever, computed by hand, released by whoever; the month's story scattered across ad-hoc transfers. Disease: the exception become the method (Ch. 7) — fraud's preferred vehicle normalised. Fix: the legitimate catalogue closed, the mini-run governance (document, machinery computation, separated hands, posting, statement), and the off-cycle count read as the ritual's health metric.</p><p><b>The fifth law.</b> The structure computes, the state rides, the run seals, the account renders — and now the one-offs join: <b>variable pay is governed, not improvised — every overtime hour through one channel, every bonus a labelled document, every commission computed from source with its statement, every correction derived and travelling forward, every loan an instrument with a visible balance, and every off-cycle a mini-run with the same hands and the same paper.</b> The variable layer is where structure leaks or where it proves itself — and the firm whose one-offs are documents has a payroll that flexes without leaking, which is the whole difference between generosity and chaos. Module 6 climbs to the layer that changes the fixed pay itself: increments, reviews, and the governance of the pay structure over time.</p>"),
]

